{"qid": "E-PMC10260929-r2__PMC10260929-r4", "kind": "E", "cell": "D", "route": "structured", "tokens_delivered": 698, "prompt_sha256": "854d2dda81f83b81fa27910568ea40ca883b68570b97c798866ef3408381e796", "row_ids": ["PMC10260929-r2", "PMC10260929-r4"], "line_ids": [], "text": "{\"verdict\": \"conditionally_comparable\", \"blockers\": [\"ionic_strength\", \"medium\"], \"citations\": [\"row:PMC10260929-r2\", \"row:PMC10260929-r4\"]}", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "input_tokens": 1357, "output_tokens": 70, "cost_usd": 0.01022875, "duration_ms": 2221, "answered_at": "2026-09-26T23:04:39+00:00"}
{"qid": "D-002", "kind": "D", "cell": "D", "route": "prose_fallback", "tokens_delivered": 7877, "prompt_sha256": "067141139c6bf13029cf2b7a2a1ab1fb30e35cf7a439389d71f276f3722b1be8", "row_ids": [], "line_ids": ["b7183438be8f0a20", "4c61c3a22d968cee", "517ac6f49b7835d4", "0c945b0a6564ff31", "a62134a1ff545f5e", "826fa423e730985a", "088a72fcbdb98f74", "e2294bedfb33c274", "abee10ba202efd96", "71b346cd6bfcc341", "9be504bda1bbbbdd", "5f7bb8fd7138b962", "7c73116a7b8da3c8", "662a55fa8401d717", "ad9a5775a7e9ddc4", "fc27342162a9e511", "4ebe26413fae1a46", "33602df042ce770b", "cb2974829b1e8af3", "e646a2143fb1ebca", "ac9a41750a3470db", "ef6f569f30184a40", "5d3fa4aad5a89e9e", "bd7bd866ac768e57", "585c39d8821da273", "c71237057c7f40a9", "5620290d10e5b9df", "82878fc9ea3b26e5", "b930a71a1006fde9", "65003e62868ae51e", "fde785b6e2c1cb68", "3334ce20991f880b", "cb693e23e652c116", "1cc480460e49d5db", "66c02bad8d6caf80", "4fc3ef2610a6cd36", "a5e832a49e1344a3", "9b169d902e42e356", "1cb6374b9f91c477", "99e8a04583ba994e"], "text": "{\"answer\": \"Divalent cations like Ca2+ are more effective at neutralizing negative surface charges and compressing the electrical double layer around plastic particles than monovalent cations, reducing electrostatic repulsion between particles and lowering the energy barrier to aggregation (PMC12376886:L46; PMC13213765:L44; PMC11543686:L77). This charge-screening effect follows the principle that the critical coagulation concentration is inversely proportional to the sixth power of ionic charge, so divalent ions exert 50–83 times more destabilizing influence than monovalent ions (PMC12376886:L46). Ca2+ also promotes cation bridging (e.g., with carboxyl groups on particle surfaces) and has a smaller hydration radius/greater charge-shielding capacity than monovalent Na+, further enhancing particle retention and aggregation (PMC11899741:L77; PMC11543686:L15). Additionally, adsorption of the divalent cation onto like-charged particle surfaces can introduce extra short-range attractive forces beyond simple double-layer compression, causing further deviation from expected stability behavior compared to monovalent salts (PMC10501195:L28).\", \"citations\": [\"PMC12376886:L46\", \"PMC13213765:L44\", \"PMC11543686:L77\", \"PMC11899741:L77\", \"PMC11543686:L15\", \"PMC10501195:L28\"]}", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "input_tokens": 11592, "output_tokens": 472, "cost_usd": 0.017605, "duration_ms": 5921, "answered_at": "2026-09-26T23:04:41+00:00"}
{"qid": "D-001", "kind": "D", "cell": "D", "route": "prose_fallback", "tokens_delivered": 8298, "prompt_sha256": "63ba8feffc3e2bb4857e6ff10effdb4f069d6e80e0eda1aeaae448ffefa17613", "row_ids": [], "line_ids": ["3851218b18a7ab79", "9dc56a3dfeea1fe6", "6ade135fbe5140e1", "132d5dd3ad4742f1", "b53a3a7fdbff87cf", "5e363e95ce57b47c", "d9309b378c9eec16", "2b69b993093621d5", "76765b9624d862e8", "d5feafc8ac4885c4", "d7e7f39166014127", "f46915b70da5abf6", "c246df41b8b81164", "eaad155c92acec7d", "6d3f0986b6663b04", "147b5d753d670713", "e920bb9a1b2ae4b4", "e3d9a4583ae6486e", "4ad81066bfb71922", "a6674543501a66ff", "8b922d594ac88cfc", "7b277b15ec6811dc", "419bf377e53f5b7b", "7e0c0fd0df6cf062", "cdee14607562b47a", "3b8a60e9b64e85ba", "fb7743a34d2f5653", "58e489fadab9e05c", "4fcb7e08aa20c40a", "b73e9ff71766db12", "815489b82932ad85", "c4e91df81e96f752", "368406d62d3ff758", "197d6d3405180278", "174a0bfaec48ddd7", "ccc8c6c9a8e74147", "6b4ffb0763221074", "4e54a2bfaadb7796", "b308faeb86ea69c1", "9bde448b607f7594"], "text": "{\"answer\": \"An eco-corona/biofilm forms when biomolecules and organic matter (e.g., humic and fulvic acids, proteins, sugars, EPS, soil metabolites) adsorb onto the plastic surface, and because these adsorbed molecules carry their own ionizable/charged groups (e.g., carboxylic groups on humic/fulvic acids), they replace the native plastic surface as the layer sampled by zeta-potential measurements, shifting the measured charge (e.g., making it more negative) (PMC10830523:L132, PMC10830523:L134). The composition and density of these charged sites differ from the bare polymer surface, altering electrostatic interactions with the surrounding medium and cells (PMC10830523:L132). More broadly, eco-corona/biofilm formation substantially alters the physicochemical surface properties of microplastics—including hydrophobicity, functional-group composition, and charge distribution—consistent with observed increases in zeta potential and changes in surface chemistry after interaction with soil metabolites (PMC13394419:L25, PMC13394419:L31). For nanoplastics coated with EPS, the corona's effect on surface charge (zeta potential) depends on the base surface chemistry and corona properties: bare/COOH-nanoplastics showed minimal zeta potential change with EPS coating, while NH2-nanoplastics showed a large charge reduction and even charge reversal with high-molecular-weight EPS, and pH had little additional effect—showing that the corona's charged functional groups and their interaction with the underlying surface groups (not just physical coating) drive the measured charge shift (PMC13057466:L43, PMC13057466:L44, PMC13057466:L19).\", \"citations\": [\"PMC10830523:L132\", \"PMC10830523:L134\", \"PMC13394419:L25\", \"PMC13394419:L31\", \"PMC13057466:L43\", \"PMC13057466:L44\", \"PMC13057466:L19\"]}", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "input_tokens": 11702, "output_tokens": 656, "cost_usd": 0.022260000000000002, "duration_ms": 7813, "answered_at": "2026-09-26T23:04:41+00:00"}
